 <section v-if="section==='learn'" class="lesson-section"><div class="section-intro"><span class="eyebrow">BUILD THE MENTAL MODEL</span><h2>Small enough to understand. Real enough to explore.</h2><p>Follow one prompt through Nuxt, Nitro, Node.js and a real Claude Agent SDK runtime.</p></div><div class="learning-grid"><article v-for="([title,text],i) in concepts" :key="title" class="learning-card"><span class="eyebrow">0{{i+1}} / LEARN</span><h3>{{title}}</h3><p>{{text}}</p></article></div><aside class="lesson-note"><strong>SDK messages are not token deltas.</strong><p>The timeline shows selected lifecycle and assistant messages. Final SDK results report turns, duration, cost and error state; unreported cost remains null.</p></aside><a class="lesson-button primary" href="#setup">Set up the application →</a></section>
 <section v-else-if="section==='setup'" class="lesson-section"><div class="section-intro"><span class="eyebrow">WINDOWS TERMINAL · POWERSHELL</span><h2>One Nuxt server. One application.</h2><p>Install Node.js 24. Local startup requires no Python, separate backend, Docker or cloud account.</p></div><article class="lesson-step"><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Install and configure this edition</h3><CodeBlock :code="setup" language="powershell"/><p>Replace the key placeholder and save. Existing environment values take precedence; restart after changes.</p><CodeBlock :code="configuration" language="ini"/><p>Leave CLAUDE_MODEL blank for the SDK default or choose a model your account can access. AGENT_MAX_TURNS accepts integers 1–10. A key-presence check does not verify credits or authentication.</p></article><article class="lesson-step"><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Run Nuxt development</h3><CodeBlock code="npm run dev" language="powershell"/><p>Open <a href="http://127.0.0.1:5900">http://127.0.0.1:5900</a>. UI and API share port 5900. Ctrl+C stops Nuxt. Read the custom <a href="/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">API reference</a> and <a href="/openapi.json">OpenAPI schema</a>.</p></article><article class="lesson-step"><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Build and start the Node server</h3><CodeBlock :code="checks" language="powershell"/><p>Stop development before npm start. Build regenerates source excerpts, compiles Nuxt/Nitro and packages the actual platform CLI into .output/server/node_modules. The local production launcher explicitly loads .env and defaults to port 5900.</p></article><article class="lesson-step"><span class="step-number">04</span><h3>Host the production output</h3><p>DEPLOYMENT.md explains deploying .output to a compatible Node 24 host or building the supplied non-root Debian Docker image. Build on the deployment operating system, inject server secrets at runtime, configure HTTPS and authentication at the intended boundary, and verify real inference separately.</p><CodeBlock code="node .output/server/index.mjs" language="bash"/><p>Direct Nitro execution reads process environment and defaults to port 3000 unless PORT/NITRO_PORT is supplied. It does not load .env automatically. Use HOST=0.0.0.0 in a container. TRUST_PROXY=1 is only for a controlled HTTPS proxy.</p></article><aside class="lesson-note"><strong>Execution has a lifetime.</strong><p>The application deadline is 180 seconds. Cancellation aborts the owned SDK work. Temporary directories and scrubbed environment variables do not provide metadata or network isolation, and this lesson does not persist conversations.</p></aside></section>
 <section v-else-if="section==='architecture'" class="lesson-section"><div class="section-intro"><span class="eyebrow">FOLLOW ONE REQUEST</span><h2>See how the pieces connect.</h2><p>Nuxt serves the Vue interface and Nitro exposes its Node API from one origin.</p></div><div class="architecture-flow" role="list"><div v-for="[label,title,text] in layers" :key="title" class="architecture-node" role="listitem"><span>{{label}}</span><strong>{{title}}</strong><p>{{text}}</p></div></div><figure class="lesson-step uml-card"><span class="eyebrow">UML SEQUENCE DIAGRAM</span><h3>From a click to a final answer</h3><p>Read downward and scroll inside the diagram to see every lifeline. Transparent loop and alternative frames explain messages, success, failure and missing configuration.</p><div class="uml-viewport" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable UML workflow"><img src="/workflow.svg" width="1680" height="1660" alt="Sixteen steps through browser, Nuxt client, Nitro routing, Node service, Claude Agent SDK runtime and Claude API."/></div><figcaption>POST SSE · TypeScript Agent SDK · private server key.</figcaption><div class="diagram-downloads"><a href="/workflow.svg" download>Download UML diagram (SVG)</a><a href="/workflow.puml" download>Download editable PlantUML source</a></div></figure><div class="section-intro"><h3>Follow every UML arrow in the code</h3><p>Each numbered step explains an excerpt generated from this edition’s actual source.</p></div><article v-for="step in workflow" :id="`uml-step-${step.number}`" :key="step.number" class="lesson-step workflow-code-step"><span class="step-number">{{step.number}}</span><h3>{{step.number}}. {{step.title}}</h3><p>{{step.text}}</p><CodeBlock :code="step.code" :language="step.language" :source="`${step.source}:${step.line}`"/><div class="workflow-code-explanation"><strong>How this code explains step {{step.number}}</strong><p>{{step.explanation}}</p></div></article><aside class="lesson-note"><strong>Completion differs from success.</strong><p>The browser requires a final result plus done. A non-success SDK subtype remains an error, and failure sends error without done.</p></aside></section>
